to the aerogel performance by consistently driving more contaminated water into the aerogel until saturation. Additionally, no leaching of MOF particles occurred during the compression process since no particle signal was detected by DLS. The compressed aerogels quickly recovered their shape when re-immersed in water (Figure 3C). Hybrid aerogels with ZIF-8 and MIL-100 were also tested for water treatment and showed a high capacity for the removal of other hazardous materials including benzotriazole and Rhodamine B, for ZIF-8 and for MIL-100(Fe) aerogels, respectively (Figure S4 and Table S2, Supporting Information).
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a facile way of combining two emerging materials – MOFs and CNCs – into one highly functional aerogel. The CNCs act as the structural component supporting the functional MOFs in a 3D flexible and lightweight networked material. The MOF loading can be tailored by changing the initial ratio of components, up to \(50\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) MOF content can be easily achieved. Three different types of MOFs were embedded into the CNC-based aerogels, highlighting the versatility of the processing method and materials, and showing that the MOFs retain their crystallinity and function. Water treatment applications were demonstrated based on the good absorption properties of aerogels, however these materials may also be extended to be used as air filters, substratesupported catalysis, and sensors, to name just a few examples.
## Experimental Section
Materials: Whatman cotton ashless filter aid was purchased from GE Healthcare Canada, and sulfuric acid \((95{-}98\%)\) was purchased from VWR Canada. Dimethyl sulfoxide (reagent grade) was purchased from Caledon Laboratory Chemicals (Georgetown, ON, Canada). Sodium CMC \((M_{\mathrm{w}}\approx250\ 000\ \mathrm{g\ mol^{-1}}\), degree of substitution \(=0.9\)), adipic acid dihydrazide (ADH, \(98\%\)), \(N\)-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS, \(97\%\)), \(N^{\prime}\)-ethyl\(N\)-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-carbodiimide (EDC, commercial grade), zinc nitrate hexahydrate \((98\%)\), 2-methylimidazole \((99\%)\), zirconium chloride
www.MaterialsViews.com
\((\geq\!99.5\%)\) , terephthalic acid (BDC, \(98\%\) ), acetate acid \((\geq\!99.7\%)\) , iron (III) chloride hexahydrate \((\ge\!98\%)\) , trimethyl 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate \((95\%)\) , benzotriazole \((99\%)\) , potassium dichromate \((\ge\!99\%)\) , and Rhodamine B \((\ge\!95\%)\) were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich and used without further purification. All water used was purified Type I water with a resistivity of 18.2 MΩ cm (Barnstead NANOpure Diamond system, Thermo Scientific, Asheville, NC, USA).
MOFs: ZIF-8, UiO-66, and MIL-100(Fe) were prepared using methods reported in the literature.[31] Briefly, ZIF-8 was prepared by dropping zinc nitrate solution \((1.17\ \mathfrak{g}\) zinc nitrate hexahydrate in \(\textbf{8g}\) water) into 2-methylimidazole solution \((22.7\ \textbf{g}\ 2\) -methylimidazole in \(80\ \textbf{g}\) water) under stirring for \(10\;\mathsf{m i n}\) and collected by ultracentrifugation and washed three times with water. UiO-66 was prepared by dissolving zirconium chloride \((915\,\mathrm{~mg})\) and BDC \((645\,\mathrm{~mg})\) in DMF \((225\,\mathrm{~mL})\) containing acetic acid \((6.7\;\mathsf{m L})\) , which was kept under \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) and collected by centrifugation and washed with methanol (three times) afterward. MIL-100(Fe) was prepared by dispersing iron (III) chloride hexahydrate \((0.162\ \textcircled{\mathrm{g}})\) and trimethyl 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate \((0.138\ \textcircled{\mathrm{g}})\) in water \((5\,\textrm{m L})\) , which was then kept under \(130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 d. The product was centrifuged and washed with acetone (three times).
Preparation of \(N H N H_{2}\) -CMC, \(N H N H_{2}{\mathrm{-}}C N C s,\) and CHO-CNCs: \(N H N H_{2}\) -CNCs and CHO-CNCs were synthesized using the method described in previous work.[2,34] Briefly, hydrazide groups were introduced onto CMC (or CNC) by reacting ADH with CMC (or CNC) through the NHS/EDC coupling system; aldehyde groups were generated by the oxidization of the CNC using \(\mathsf{N a l O4}\) . Solutions/suspensions were stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) before further experiments.
Preparation of Hybrid Aerogels: MOF nanoparticles were suspended into 1 wt% CHO-CNC suspensions, with 15 min of probe sonication. The resulting suspensions were mixed with an equal volume of \(1\ \mathsf{w t}\%\) \(N H N H_{2}\) -CMC, or \(N H N H_{2}\) -CNCs, using a vortex mixer (Level 8, Analog Vortex Mixer, VWR) for 2 min. It was noted that MOFs needed to be premixed with colloidally stable CNCs first and then the CMC was added, if MOFs were added to CMC first, precipitation was observed. All suspensions were in ultrapure water without pH adjustment and the native pH of CHO-CNC suspensions and \(N H N H_{2}\) -CMC solutions was between 5 and 6. The final suspension of crosslinked clusters was transferred into a cylindrical glass vial ( \(14.5\;\mathsf{m m}\) in diameter) and allowed to set for another 10 min before transfer into the freezer \((-20\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . The final hybrid aerogels were obtained by freeze-drying the ice-gel.
Adsorption: Adsorption kinetics were studied by soaking the aerogels or MOF particles in \(10\,\textrm{m L}\) of aqueous solution containing different contaminants \((200\;\mathsf{m g}\;\mathsf{L}^{-1}\) benzo triazole for ZIF-8, \(10\;\mathsf{m g}\;\mathsf{L}^{-1}\) potassium) dichromate for UiO-66, and \(0.01\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}\) Rhodamine B for MIL-100(Fe) for a predetermined time. Then the solution was analyzed by UV–vis to determine the concentration based on a calibration curve prepared from solutions with known contaminant concentrations. The adsorption capacity at time \(t\) was calculated using the following equation:
\[
q_{t}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{t})V}{m}
\]
where \(V\) was the solution volume, \(m\) was the mass of the aerogel or the mass of MOF particles, \(C_{0}\) was the initial concentration, \(C_{t}\) was the concentration at time \(t.\) . The time-dependence of the adsorption was fitted with the pseudo-second-order kinetic model, which was:
\[
\frac{t}{q_t}=\frac{1}{k_2q_e^2}+\frac{1}{q_e}t
\]
where \(q_{t}\) and \(q_{e}\) were the adsorption capacities at time \(t\) and equilibrium, respectively, and \(k_{2}\) was the rate constant of the pseudo-second-order model.
Characterization: JEOL JSM 7000 SEM was used to characterize the MOF particles and aerogels with a \(5\ \mathsf{n m}\) platinum coating. PXRD was conducted on a Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer with a scan speed of \(1^{\circ}\ m i n^{-1}\) , a step size of \(0.05^{\circ}\) , and a 2θ range of \(2{-}50^{\circ}\) .
The concentration of the contaminated water was determined using a DU 800 UV–vis spectrophotometer. The UiO-66 nanoparticle size distribution (Figure S6, Supporting Information) was obtained by measuring 100 randomly selected nanoparticles using the Nano Measure software, giving an average size of \(180\pm\:30\:\:\mathsf{n m}\) from SEM analysis. The presence of MOFs in the purified water test (leaching) was determined using a Malvern Zetasizer Nanoparticle Analyzer (dynamic light scattering apparatus) at \(25~^{\circ}\subset\) . TGA was performed on a TA Q5000 thermogravimetric analyzer with a ramp rate of \(10~^{\circ}\subset\) min–1 up to \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and then holding at that temperature for \(10\ \mathsf{m i n}\) . ICP-OES was conducted using a Varian ICP-OES Vista Pro.
